course: religion-ethics
lesson: 80
title: Life and death: putting it all together
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In the last lesson of the block Mrs Patel asks each child for one thing they have learned about life, and Ellie says, «That life is precious, even for a hamster»: every life, big or small, is a gift worth caring for.
explain: The children's answers gather the whole journey: it is okay to be very sad when a pet dies; to a friend whose parent died you can simply say «I'm sorry» and then be a normal friend; funerals are less scary once you know what happens; and grief comes back on birthdays and holidays, in waves that slowly get gentler.
explain: Max says he is less scared of dying since talking about it, because a fear that is shared gets smaller; Ellie says different people believe different things about what happens after death, Christians resurrection and heaven, others rebirth, judgement and Paradise, or that life simply ends, and friends can respect each other across all of them.
explain: Lily asks, «Will I ever stop missing Mum?», and the honest answer is that she may always miss her a little, but the pain softens and happy memories grow; Christians believe love never ends and that God will one day «wipe away every tear».
explain: Mrs Patel ends with a prayer, «Thank you, God, for life, for love, and for the people we remember», and the children who do not pray sit quietly, giving thanks in their own way: everyone joins in, each as themselves.
explain: For a child today this means: love the living, remember the dead, look after one another, and always tell an adult straight away if a friend is in danger.
fact: Every life, big or small, is valuable and worth caring for.
fact: Grief for a pet is real and normal; it shows love.
fact: To a friend whose parent died, simple honest words like «I'm sorry», and staying a normal friend, help most.
fact: Knowing what happens at a funeral helps children feel less afraid.
fact: Grief often returns on birthdays, anniversaries and holidays; this is normal.
fact: Sharing fears about death makes them smaller.
fact: Christians believe in resurrection and heaven; Muslims in judgement and Paradise; Hindus, Sikhs and Buddhists in rebirth; humanists that life ends at death; people of different beliefs can respect each other and be friends.
fact: Visiting old and lonely people is a great kindness.
fact: People may always miss someone they loved a little, but the pain usually softens and happy memories grow.
fact: Christians believe love never ends and that God will one day «wipe away every tear».
fact: Everyone can join a moment of thanks in their own way: praying, thinking or simply being quiet.
fact: If a friend says they wish they were dead, tell an adult straight away; Childline is 0800 1111.
fact: Grief has no single right shape and no deadline.
exercise: Ellie says life is precious «even for a hamster». Is that a good answer? | Yes; every life, big or small, is worth caring for | No; only human lives matter | No; small animals don't feel anything | Yes, but only if it is an expensive pet
exercise: Oscar learned it's okay to be very sad when a pet dies. Why? | Grief for a pet is real and shows love | Pets are worth much more than people, so their deaths matter more | It's only okay if the pet was big | Crying for pets is just practice
exercise: What did Mia learn to say to a friend whose parent died? | «I'm sorry», and then just be a normal friend | «I know exactly how you feel, because my goldfish died last year» | «At least you've still got one parent» | Nothing; avoid them until they're better
exercise: Why does Callum find funerals less scary now? | He knows what happens at them | Funerals have become much shorter | He won't ever have to go to one | Funerals are now held only for pets
exercise: Holly learned that grief can return on birthdays and holidays. What is true? | It's normal for grief to come back on special days | It means she is grieving the wrong way | Grief should be over before the first birthday | It only happens to people who didn't cry
exercise: Why is Max less scared of dying now? | Talking about his fear made it smaller | He found out from the vicar that he will never die | He watched lots and lots of scary films to get brave | He stopped thinking about anything at all, ever
exercise: Ellie learned that people believe different things about death. What follows? | People can respect each other and be friends | Only one group should be allowed in class | Everyone must believe the same as Ellie | Beliefs about death don't matter at all
exercise: Which belief is matched to the right group? | Humanists: life ends at death | Hindus: the Day of Judgement only | Muslims: rebirth as animals | Christians: becoming ghosts
exercise: Freddie wants to visit his nan more often. Why is that good? | Visiting the old and lonely is a great kindness | Nan will give him more pocket money if he comes more | Visiting is the law for all grandchildren | It will stop Nan from ever getting old
exercise: Lily asks, «Will I ever stop missing Mum?» What is the honest answer? | Maybe never completely, but it will feel softer | Yes, by next month she'll forget all about it and feel completely fine again | No, it will hurt exactly the same for ever | Only if she stops looking at photos
exercise: What do Christians believe God will one day do? | «Wipe away every tear» | Make everyone forget the dead | Bring all pets back as people | Stop everyone from loving
exercise: Mrs Patel ends with a prayer. What do the children who don't pray do? | Sit quietly and give thanks in their own way | Leave the room, because they aren't allowed to stay | Pretend to pray with the others so that nobody notices | Talk loudly all the way through to show they disagree
exercise: Which feelings can grief include? | Sadness, numbness, anger, guilt and even laughter | Only sadness, and only on the funeral day | Only anger, for exactly one year | Only happiness, once the funeral is done
exercise: A friend writes that she wishes she were dead. What do you do? | Tell an adult straight away; Childline is 0800 1111 | Keep her secret, because she asked you to and she trusts you | Tell her she's being silly | Wait until she says it again
exercise: Which is the wisest sentence to sum up this block? | Love the living, remember the dead, look after each other | Never think or talk about death at all | Only grown-ups need to understand death | Death means nothing, so nothing matters
exercise: How long does grief last? | There is no deadline; it usually softens over time | Exactly one week, then it stops | Exactly one year, then it stops for good and you feel happy again | It never changes at all
test: «Crying over a goldfish? That's peak cringe,» says a Gen Alpha boy. Which answer best uses what the class learned? | Grief for any pet is real; it shows love | He's right; fish don't count | Only cry for pets bigger than a cat | Crying for pets is practice for real grief
test: «His dad died, so I'll just avoid him till he's normal again,» says a lad in Leeds. What's the better plan? | Say «I'm sorry», then just be his normal mate | Avoid him, he wants to be alone for ever | Ask him lots of questions about how it happened | Tell him he'll get over it soon
test: Mrs Patel says, very formally, at the end: «Let us give thanks, each according to his or her own conscience.» What does she mean? | Everyone can give thanks in their own way | Only Christians may stay in the room for the end of the lesson | Everyone must say the very same prayer out loud together | Nobody should give thanks today, because it is a sad topic
test: What would happen if Holly expected that grief might come back on Mum's birthday? | She'd be less shocked, and could plan something special | It would stop grief coming back at all | It would make the day much worse | She would forget the birthday entirely
test: «We're all gonna die anyway, so stop visiting your nan, she's old,» says a rough cousin. Which reasoning is best? | Because life is short, visits matter more, not less | He's right; visits are pointless for old people, because they'll die soon anyway | Visiting only matters if Nan is rich | Nan won't notice who visits her
test: «Will I ever stop missin' me mam?» asks Lily in a Belfast accent. Which answer is honest and kind? | You may always miss her a bit, but it'll get softer | Yes, in a week it'll all be gone and you won't miss her at all | No, it'll hurt the same for ever | Only if you stop talking about her
test: «Priya believes in rebirth, Ellie in heaven, Jack's uncle in nothing after. So one of them's a muppet,» says a boy in Wigan (muppet = fool). What's the best response? | They disagree, but nobody's a fool; respect them all | He's right; two of them must be stupid, because only one can be right | The teacher should decide who wins | They should stop being friends
test: In a cartoon, a kid learns «death is nothing to be scared of» in one episode and never feels afraid again. What's more true? | Fears come and go, and talking helps each time | One talk fixes fear for ever | Being scared again means you failed the lesson and have to start all over | Only cartoons have fears about death
test: «My mate said he don't wanna be alive no more, but he's probs joking,» says a boy in Sydney (probs = probably). What should he do? | Tell an adult straight away anyway | Ignore it if it sounded like a joke | Laugh so his mate feels better | Keep it secret to be loyal
test: Ellie says life is precious «even for a hamster», and the vicar replies, formally, «All creatures great and small are held in God's regard.» What do they agree on? | Every living thing deserves care | Hamsters are more important than people | Only big animals matter to God | Pets must be taken to church
test: «So is death sad or is it just a normal part of life?» asks a thoughtful girl. Which answer is wisest? | Both: it's natural, and losing someone is still sad | Only sad; nature never includes death | Only normal; nobody should ever feel sad | Neither; death doesn't really happen
test: Myth to check: «Once the funeral's over, grief is over.» | False; grief comes in waves and softens slowly | True; funerals end all grief for good, for everyone | True, but only for grown-ups, not for small children | False; grief always gets worse every single year
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the course of grief as presented throughout this block of lessons? | It occurs in waves, recurs upon significant dates, and commonly diminishes over time | It ceases entirely upon the conclusion of the funeral | It persists undiminished in perpetuity | It is confined to the loss of human relatives
exam: Identify the conduct most consistent with the matters taught in respect of a classmate who has lost a parent. | A brief expression of sympathy followed by ordinary friendship | Avoidance of the classmate until the grief has passed entirely and he is cheerful again | Detailed enquiry into the circumstances of the death | Assurance that the loss will shortly be forgotten
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning differing beliefs regarding death. | Persons of differing faiths and of none may hold their views and nonetheless respect one another | All persons are obliged to hold a single belief | Beliefs differing from one's own ought to be ridiculed in public, so that error may be exposed and corrected | Beliefs concerning death are of no consequence to anyone
exam: Whereas a closing prayer is offered in a class of mixed belief, ascertain the arrangement most consistent with the matters taught. | Each pupil participates according to conscience, whether by prayer or by quiet reflection | Pupils who do not pray are required to leave the room | All pupils are compelled to recite the prayer aloud, irrespective of their own belief or conscience | The prayer is abandoned owing to the presence of non-believers
